=> Introduction to Trees
    -> Tree
        -> A non-linear data structure that organizes nodes in a hierarchical parent-child relationship.
        -> A rooted tree has one root; every other node has exactly one parent.
        -> A tree contains no cycles.

    -> Node
        -> An individual element of a tree.

    -> Edge
        -> A connection between two nodes.
        -> For N nodes, a tree has N - 1 edges.

    -> Root Node
        -> The topmost node, which has no parent.

    -> Parent and Child
        -> Parent: A node directly above and connected to another node.
        -> Child: A node directly below and connected to its parent.

    -> Internal Node
        -> A node with at least one child.
        -> The root can also be an internal node.

    -> Leaf Node
        -> A node with no children.
        -> Its height is 0.

    -> Siblings
        -> Nodes sharing the same immediate parent.

    -> Ancestors
        -> All nodes on the path from the root to a node, excluding the node itself.
        -> Includes the immediate parent.

    -> Descendants
        -> All nodes reachable below a node by following child connections.
        -> Includes children, grandchildren, etc.

    -> Depth
        -> Number of edges from the root to a given node.
        -> Root depth = 0.

    -> Level
        -> Indicates the layer of a node.
        -> When root level = 1, Level = Depth + 1.
        -> Some books use root level = 0.

    -> Height
        -> Number of edges on the longest downward path from a node to a leaf.
        -> Leaf height = 0.
        -> Tree height = height of its root.

    -> Breadth-First Search (BFS)
        -> Visits nodes level by level, from left to right.
        -> Also called Level-Order Traversal.

    -> Depth-First Search (DFS)
        -> Explores one branch before moving to another.
        -> Preorder visits the current node before its children.
        -> For ordered children, preorder visits children from left to right.
        -> Other DFS variations include inorder and postorder.

    -> Example Tree
        ->             A
        ->           /   \
        ->          B     C
        ->         / \     \
        ->        D   E     F
        ->           /
        ->          G
        -> Root = A
        -> Internal nodes = A, B, C, E
        -> Leaf nodes = D, F, G
        -> Siblings = B and C; D and E
        -> Ancestors of G = A, B, E
        -> Descendants of B = D, E, G
        -> Depth of E = 2
        -> Height of B = 2
        -> Tree height = 3
        -> BFS = A, B, C, D, E, F, G
        -> DFS preorder = A, B, D, E, G, C, F

    -> Practical Java Backend Example
        -> Company departments can form a parent-child hierarchy.
        -> Each Department object represents a tree node.
        -> A List<Department> can store references to child departments.
        -> A department without subdepartments is a leaf node.